\title[Boundary geometry of the Berggren tree]
      {The Boundary Geometry of the Berggren Tree:\\
       Horocyclic Stars, Charge Quantization, and Escape Rates}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{8 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Plotting the Berggren (Barning--Hall) ternary tree of primitive Pythagorean triples
inside a disc, by mapping a triple $(a,b,c)$ to the direction $(a/c,b/c)$ on the boundary
circle at a radius increasing with depth, produces a picture with two conspicuous and
unexplained visual features: streaks that behave like radii of the disc, and --- far more
striking --- dense bundles of curves converging on isolated points \emph{of the boundary
circle itself}, which look like stars embedded in the rim.

We give a complete algebro-geometric account of this picture. The Pythagorean equation is
the light cone of the quadratic form $Q(a,b,c)=a^2+b^2-c^2$ of signature $(2,1)$, the
three Berggren generators lie in the integral Lorentz group $O(2,1;\Z)$, and the plotting
map is the ideal-point map of the Klein model of the hyperbolic plane.

We prove: a \emph{chord--charge identity} expressing the squared chordal distance between
two plotted ideal points as $-2\ip{v}{p}/(c_vc_p)$; a \emph{star theorem}, that any family
of triples of constant Lorentz charge at a fixed null vector $p$ with unbounded hypotenuse
converges in the disc to $\dirp p$; an \emph{exact tangency law}, that
$c_v\|\dirp v-\dirp p\|^2$ is constant along a horocycle, pinning the contact order with
the boundary circle at exactly two; a \emph{charge quantization theorem}, that the charge
of a primitive triple at a rational ideal point is twice a square or an odd square, with
an exact spectrum; an \emph{escape-rate dichotomy}, comparing the polynomial rate
$\Theta(k^{-2})$ of the two parabolic generators with the exponential rate $O(3^{-k})$ of
the hyperbolic one, together with an irrationality obstruction showing the hyperbolic
limit is never occupied by a triple; \emph{star location and multiplicity}, that every
primitive rational ideal point with odd first leg carries a star with infinitely many
distinct spokes; an \emph{exact tree spectrum} $\{2n^2:n\ge1\}$ at $(1,0)$; and a
\emph{two-sided Lyapunov bound} $5\cdot3^{\#_B(g)}\le c(g)\le 5\cdot7^{|g|}$ forcing
exponential escape to be equivalent to a positive density of one letter in the address.
\end{abstract}

